.\" The version in the .TH line is the footer every reader sees; keep it equal
.\" to [workspace.package] version in Cargo.toml, stamped with the recipes.
.TH CARREL 1 "2026-09-01" "carrel 2026.9.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
carrel \- a quiet place to read your markdown
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B carrel
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B carrel
.I FILE
.RI [ PATTERN ]
.br
.B cmd | carrel
.RB [ \- " [" PATTERN ]]
.br
.B carrel \-\-plain
.IR FILE " [" WIDTH ]
.br
.B carrel \-\-plain \-
.RI [ WIDTH ]
.br
.B carrel \-\-render
.RI { FILE | \- "} [" WIDTH ]
.br
.B carrel \-\-tasks
.I FILE
.br
.B carrel
.RB [ \-\-diff | \-\-no\-diff | \-\-no\-mouse ]
.I FILE
.br
.B carrel \-\-help
.br
.B carrel \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B carrel
is a markdown reader \(em not an editor, not a workbench, a reading desk.
.PP
Run with no arguments it lists the markdown files around you, newest first,
with type\-to\-filter and a directory picker. Given a
.I FILE
it opens a reader with vim motions, incremental search that survives reflow and
terminal resize, syntax highlighting, tables, images, and mermaid diagrams.
Prose is set to a comfortable measure and centred rather than stretched across a
wide terminal; tables, code and diagrams still use the full width.
.PP
Given a
.I FILE
and a
.IR PATTERN ,
it prints matching lines and exits.
.PP
Piped input opens the reader too:
.B git show HEAD:README.md | carrel
streams the document in as it arrives, so a slow producer paints
immediately and your position and search matches hold while content lands.
.B carrel \-
forces stdin mode; with stdout also piped the document renders as plain
text instead, so pipelines pass through.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Render the document as linear plain text on stdout, for screen readers and for
pipes. A bare pipe selects this automatically, so
.B carrel doc.md | less
does the right thing. Output is ASCII\-safe and contains no escape bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Read the input as a unified diff even if its name does not say so: a heading
per commit and per file, hunks as code. A pipe and a
.I .diff
or
.I .patch
file are read this way already; a
.I .md
file never is.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diff
Never adapt a diff, even on a pipe.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Do not capture the mouse. Carrel is click\-first and captures the pointer by
default; this hands it back, so the terminal's own selection, scrollback and
context menu behave exactly as they do in any other program. Every action
remains reachable from the keyboard. The
.B mouse
key in the configuration file does the same thing for every run; the flag wins
for this one.
.TP
.B \-\-render
Styled linear text for embedding in another tool's output: weight, slant,
strike and OSC 8 hyperlinks, but never an SGR colour. With NO_COLOR set it
is exactly
.BR \-\-plain .
.TP
.B \-\-tasks
Print every GFM task in the file as checkbox lines and exit. The file is
never modified.
.TP
.B \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH KEYS
.SS Reader
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " \(ua ", " \(da
Scroll by a line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-E ", " Ctrl\-Y
Scroll by a line, the same as
.BR j ", " k .
.TP
.BR Space ", " b ", " PgDn ", " PgUp ", " Ctrl\-F ", " Ctrl\-B
Page forward and back.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-D ", " Ctrl\-U
Half a page forward and back.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G ", " Home ", " End
Jump to the start or the end.
.TP
.B 42G
Jump to a row by number.
.TP
.B { }
Move to the previous or next block.
.TP
.B zz zt zb
Put the current match in the middle, at the top, or at the bottom.
.TP
.BR / ", " ?
Search forward and backward. Matches survive reflow and resize by
construction.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Next and previous match.
.TP
.B o
Outline picker.
.TP
.B t
Toggle wide tables between card view and wrapping in place.
.TP
.B r
Toggle rendered blocks \(em diagrams and math \(em between art and source.
.TP
.B ] [
Move to the next or previous code block. The one in focus is marked in the
margin.
.TP
.B X
Jump to the next task-list item, wrapping. A count jumps that many.
.TP
.B y
Copy the focused code block, without its fence. With nothing focused, the
nearest code block at or after the top of the screen.
.TP
.B F
Follow a document that is still arriving on a pipe: the view stays at the end
as content appears. Any deliberate move away detaches it.
.TP
.B A
Auto\-read: the view drifts down a row at a time until any motion stops it.
.TP
.B L
What links here \(em the documents that link to this one, by
.I [[wikilink]]
or by ordinary markdown link. Enter opens one.
.TP
.B l
What this points at \(em every destination in the document, the mirror of
.BR L .
Enter opens a local one; an external URL is copied to the clipboard. Carrel
neither fetches it nor launches anything to open it.
.TP
.B m
Set or clear a bookmark at the current position. Bookmarks are remembered
between sessions and survive a resize; they do not survive an edit to the file.
.TP
.B '
Go to the next bookmark, wrapping at the end.
.TP
.B \(dq
List every bookmark with its line; enter jumps to one.
.RB ( q " or " Esc
closes this pane, and the two link panes below.)
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab
Select the next or previous link.
.TP
.B Enter
Follow the selected link. A markdown file beside this one opens in the reader;
any destination with a scheme \(em a URL, a
.B mailto:
address \(em is copied to the clipboard instead, to paste wherever you meant it
to go. A reader does not spawn programs.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-O
Go back where you came from.
.TP
.B T
Cycle themes. On an Omarchy desktop the rotation includes
.BR omarchy ,
the palette derived from the theme the rest of the desktop is wearing.
.TP
.BR h ", " F1
Help.
.TP
.B H
Fold the hint footer.
.TP
.B B
Hide or show the breadcrumb band (the enclosing sections, pinned atop the
page while you scroll).
.TP
.B I
Show or hide the document card: words, reading time, structure, links,
when the file last changed.
.TP
.B S
Spotlight: dim every paragraph except the one nearest the centre of the
view.
.TP
.BR za ", " zM ", " zR
Fold the current section behind its heading; fold every section; open all.
A
.B <details>
block folds the same way behind its summary. Clicking a heading or a
summary toggles its fold. Search and links unfold their targets.
.TP
.B %
Jump to the footnote definition of the reference nearest above the view;
inside a definition, back to its first reference.
Ctrl\-O returns.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the selection, the selected link, and any search highlights.
.TP
.B q
Close the file, returning to the home screen if it was opened from there;
otherwise quit.
.TP
.BR Q ", " Ctrl\-C
Quit outright, whatever is open.
.SS Mouse
The reader takes the mouse, so the terminal's own selection is not available
while it is up. What replaces it copies the document's text and nothing else:
no scrollbar column, no fold markers, no margin outline.
.TP
.B drag
Select. The selection is copied when the button comes up, so there is no
second gesture to remember.
.TP
.BR click ", " double\-click ", " triple\-click
One click sets where a selection starts, two select the word under the
pointer, three the whole block. A click that never became a drag lands where
it fell: on a heading, or on a
.B <details>
summary, it folds that section \(em the same toggle
.B za
gives. Where the margin outline is on, a click in its columns jumps to that
section instead of starting a selection.
.TP
.B link
Click a link, wherever it is painted \(em no need to select it first. A local
markdown file opens in the reader; a URL is copied to the clipboard.
.TP
.B fold marker
The
.BR \[u25B8] / \[u25BE]
in the left margin is a button: click it to fold or unfold that section, or
that
.B <details>
block. It folds exactly what a click on the heading beside it folds.
.TP
.B pane row
In the outline, the bookmark list and the two link panes, a click opens the row
under the pointer \(em not the row the keyboard cursor was resting on. A click
anywhere else on an open pane does nothing: while a pane is up it takes every
click inside it, so nothing reaches the document behind it.
.TP
.B right\-click
Open a menu. In the document it is a menu for whatever is under the pointer
\(em a heading offers folding, a link offers opening and copying, a code block
offers copying, a table offers the card view \(em and it is positioned before
it opens, so its rows act on what was clicked rather than on wherever the view
happens to be. Anywhere else \(em the status row, the hint footer, the
breadcrumb, the margin, the scrollbar, or the home screen \(em it opens the
global menu instead.
.TP
.B menu button
The
.B \[u2261]
at the right end of the status row opens the same global menu with a left
click, for a reader who has no reason to guess that right\-clicking does
anything.
.TP
.B menu
Inside a menu:
.BR \(ua / \(da
move,
.B Enter
chooses,
.B Esc
or
.B q
closes, and a click outside closes without choosing. Moving the pointer over a
row lights it, so the keyboard cursor follows the hand. Nothing is lit when a
menu opens. Each row shows the key that does the same thing, which is how a
menu teaches its own shortcuts; a row with no key is one only the menu offers.
A greyed row says what carrel could do here and declines \(em
.B Follow the end
on a document that is not growing, or
.B Back
with nothing behind you.
.TP
.B wheel
Scroll. Three lines a notch, and more when the notches keep arriving \(em spin
it and the page keeps up, to twelve lines a notch. A pause, or a change of
direction, drops straight back to three, so a correction stays precise.
.TP
.B scrollbar
Dragging the thumb tracks the pointer and keeps hold of the point on the thumb
you took it by, so the view does not jump when the hand lands. Clicking the
empty track pages one screen toward the click rather than teleporting there.
.TP
.B lamp
The lamp on the bottom row is a switch: click it to fold the hint footer away,
or to bring it back \(em the same as
.BR H .
.SS Home screen
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " \(ua ", " \(da
Move down and up the list.
.BR Ctrl\-N / Ctrl\-P
and
.BR Ctrl\-J / Ctrl\-K
do the same while you are typing in the filter, the search or the picker,
where the plain letters are part of what you are typing.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G ", " Home ", " End
The first or the last document.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the selected document.
.TP
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " 3
Continue reading \(em reopen one of the three most recent documents at the
line you left it on.
.TP
.B T
Cycle themes.
.TP
.B H
Hide or show the key hints.
.TP
.BR h ", " F1
This help.
.B F1
works from any mode;
.B h
is a letter, so it only works when you are not typing.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the filter, then leave it. In the picker it clears the typed path first
and closes the picker only when there is nothing left to clear.
.TP
.B i
Filter by name; type to narrow, escape to leave. The match is fuzzy \(em an
in\-order subsequence, ranked best\-first \(em as it is in the outline picker.
.TP
.B /
Search inside every file; enter opens at the match.
.TP
.B d
Choose a directory. The input opens on the directory
.B carrel
was run from \(em highlight and all, so enter alone reads where you are \(em and
\fBlive\fR and \fB/live\fR both mean a subdirectory of it rather than a path
from the filesystem root. A saved
.B root
does not change that: the working directory is what \fBd\fR means by "here".
\fBEsc\fR clears the input back to empty, which is how an unrelated absolute
path is typed and how the remembered places are reached; they lead the empty
menu. Typing completes against the filesystem;
arrows, \fBCtrl\-J\fR/\fBCtrl\-K\fR or \fBCtrl\-N\fR/\fBCtrl\-P\fR move,
enter chooses. Typing owns the letters here, so \fBj\fR and \fBk\fR are
part of the path.
.TP
.B Mouse
Click a file to select it, double\-click to open it. The same works in the
directory picker, and on the results of a file search.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.IR "carrel FILE PATTERN" ,
at least one match.
.TP
.B 1
A file that could not be read, an unknown or misplaced option, a
.RI [ W ]
argument that is not a column count, or a search that found nothing \(em so
.B if carrel doc.md pattern; then
behaves as it does with grep.
.TP
.B 128+N
Killed by signal N. The terminal is restored first.
.PP
.B \-\-
ends the options, so
.B carrel \-\- ./\-weird.md
opens a file whose name begins with a dash. An unknown single\-dash flag is
ignored when stdin is a pipe, because git hands its pager flags it does not
expect to be understood; interactively it is an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Honoured: colours off, weight and emphasis kept.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " HOME
Where the config, the reading positions and the index cache live; see
.BR FILES .
Each falls back to the usual place under
.B HOME
when it is unset or empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/carrel/config
One
.I key = value
per line. Carrel writes it itself when you change a setting in the app, and it
is safe to edit by hand: unknown keys and
.B #
comments are ignored, so a config written for another version still opens. Of
a key repeated by hand the FIRST line wins, except
.BR place ,
where every line counts. The eight keys are
.RS
.TP
.B max_width
The reading measure in columns \(em prose wraps here and centres on the page.
Default 90; 0 turns it off and lets prose fill the terminal. Any other value
below 20 is raised to 20, a measure narrower than that being unreadable rather
than merely narrow. Tables, code, images and diagrams ignore it and use the
full width.
.TP
.B theme
The palette, by name:
.BR terminal ", " carrel\-dark ", " carrel\-light ", " catppuccin\-mocha ", "
.BR catppuccin\-latte ", " gruvbox\-dark ", " gruvbox\-light ", " tokyo\-night ", "
.BR nord ", " dracula ", " solarized\-dark ", " solarized\-light ", " everforest ", "
.BR rose\-pine ", " kanagawa ", " synthwave ", " oceanic .
.B dark
and
.B light
are aliases for the two carrel palettes, and
.B omarchy
is offered where the desktop publishes one (below).
.B terminal
is the default and inherits the terminal's own colours. A name carrel does not
know is not an error: it opens on
.B terminal
and says so.
.B T
cycles them and saves the choice.
.TP
.B hints
The lamplight hint row along the bottom. Default true;
.B H
toggles it.
.TP
.B titles
Show each document's own title on the home screen \(em
.B title:
from its frontmatter, else its first heading \(em instead of its file name.
Default false; a file with neither keeps its name.
.TP
.B breadcrumb
The section path pinned atop the page while you scroll, with a rule under it.
Default true;
.B B
toggles it. A document with no headings never shows one.
.TP
.B outline_margin
The section tree pinned in the left margin, the current section lit, on
terminals wide enough to spare the columns. Default false, because it moves
the text column.
.TP
.B mouse
Capture the mouse, so clicks reach carrel rather than the terminal. Default
true \(em carrel is a click\-first reader. Set false to hand the pointer back,
and the terminal's own selection, scrollback and context menu behave as they do
anywhere else.
.B \-\-no\-mouse
does the same for one run.
.TP
.B root
The directory the home screen lists.
.B d
picks one in the app.
.TP
.B place
A remembered favourite root, offered by the directory picker. This key
repeats, newest first, capped at eight; choosing a root with
.B d
records it.
.RE
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/omarchy/current/theme/colors.toml
Read, never written. Where Omarchy publishes the active desktop theme as a
terminal palette, carrel derives a
.B omarchy
palette from it and uses it as the default when no
.B theme
is on record. Re\-read once a second while that palette is the active one, so
.B omarchy theme set
restyles an open reader without a restart; carrel does not read the file at
all while you are on a fixed palette.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/carrel
Two tab\-separated files, written atomically so a crash cannot truncate them.
.B positions
holds reading positions, so a document reopens where you left it;
.B bookmarks
holds the marks
.B m
sets. Both keep the 500 most recent documents and drop the oldest beyond that
\(em they are a convenience, not a database. A tab or a newline in a path is
escaped, so no filename can corrupt either file.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/carrel
The file index: paths and modification times, nothing else. One
.BI index\-1\- hash
file per root ever opened, the 32 most recent kept. The
.B 1
is a format version, so a change to the format invalidates the caches
deliberately rather than by accident.
.SH PRIVACY
carrel reads only the directory you point it at and sends nothing anywhere.
Remote images in documents are never fetched; they render as their alt text.
